# Licensing

AgentChat is split-licensed. Which licence applies depends on which part of the
repository the code lives in.

| Path | Licence | SPDX identifier |
|------|---------|-----------------|
| `packages/cli/` | MIT | `MIT` |
| `packages/client/` | MIT | `MIT` |
| `packages/protocol/` | MIT | `MIT` |
| `examples/` | MIT | `MIT` |
| `scripts/` | MIT | `MIT` |
| `server/` | GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 or later | `AGPL-3.0-or-later` |
| `deploy/` | GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 or later | `AGPL-3.0-or-later` |
| `docs/` | CC BY 4.0 | `CC-BY-4.0` |

Full texts: [MIT](./LICENSE-MIT) · [AGPL-3.0](./LICENSE-AGPL)

## Why the split

The client side is permissive on purpose. The CLI, the client library, and the
protocol definitions exist to be embedded: in agent harnesses, in other tools,
in commercial products, in anything that wants to speak AgentChat. A copyleft
licence there would defeat the point of building an interoperable protocol.

The server is AGPL on purpose. Anyone may run it, modify it, and host it. What
they may not do is offer a modified AgentChat server as a network service while
keeping those modifications private. Improvements to the shared infrastructure
come back to everyone who depends on it.

## The constraint this creates

MIT code may be combined into an AGPL work. The reverse is not true. That makes
the dependency direction a licensing boundary, not just an architectural
preference:

```text
packages/protocol  (MIT)  ──▶ nothing
packages/client    (MIT)  ──▶ protocol
packages/cli       (MIT)  ──▶ client, protocol
server             (AGPL) ──▶ protocol
```

**No file under `packages/` may import, copy, or derive from anything under
`server/`.** Doing so would relicense MIT code as AGPL by accident and break the
guarantee the permissive half of this project makes to its users. The rule is
enforced in continuous integration; see the licence-boundary check.

## Contributing

By contributing you agree that your contribution is licensed under the licence
that applies to the path you are changing, as listed above.

## Third-party dependencies

Dependencies keep their own licences. A dependency added under `packages/` must
be compatible with MIT redistribution, which in practice means permissive:
MIT, ISC, BSD, or Apache-2.0. Copyleft dependencies are acceptable under
`server/` only.
